1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,504
What if everything you learned about the

2
00:00:02,504 --> 00:00:04,540
last three hundred years was a lie?

3
00:00:05,190 --> 00:00:07,815
What if, not long ago, there was an empire

4
00:00:07,815 --> 00:00:10,148
so vast it covered much of the Earth,

5
00:00:10,798 --> 00:00:13,482
with domed palaces, star-shaped citadels,

6
00:00:13,482 --> 00:00:16,165
and towers that drew energy from the sky?

7
00:00:16,815 --> 00:00:19,268
And what if it was wiped from history so

8
00:00:19,268 --> 00:00:21,721
completely that today, almost no one has

9
00:00:21,721 --> 00:00:22,747
heard its name?

10
00:00:23,397 --> 00:00:25,304
Its name, they say, was Tartaria.

11
00:00:25,954 --> 00:00:28,148
Millions of people have watched videos

12
00:00:28,148 --> 00:00:30,288
about it. Some are convinced it's the

13
00:00:30,288 --> 00:00:31,679
greatest cover-up ever.

14
00:00:32,329 --> 00:00:34,417
Tonight, we'll follow the clues, all of

15
00:00:34,417 --> 00:00:36,057
them, and see where they lead.

16
00:00:36,707 --> 00:00:39,032
If you love a good mystery, and finding

17
00:00:39,032 --> 00:00:41,468
out what's really behind it, subscribe.

18
00:00:41,468 --> 00:00:43,351
It genuinely helps the channel.

19
00:00:44,001 --> 00:00:46,166
The first clue is real, and you can check

20
00:00:46,166 --> 00:00:46,904
it yourself.

21
00:00:47,554 --> 00:00:51,302
Open old European maps from the 1500s to

22
00:00:51,302 --> 00:00:54,440
the 1700s, and you'll find a huge

23
00:00:54,440 --> 00:00:58,274
territory across Asia labeled Tartaria,

24
00:00:58,274 --> 00:01:00,192
or Great Tartary.

25
00:01:00,842 --> 00:01:03,554
An eighteenth-century encyclopedia even

26
00:01:03,554 --> 00:01:06,266
describes Tartary as "a vast country in

27
00:01:06,266 --> 00:01:08,268
the northern parts of Asia."

28
00:01:08,918 --> 00:01:10,110
So believers ask:

29
00:01:10,110 --> 00:01:12,553
if Tartary was on the maps, and in the

30
00:01:12,553 --> 00:01:14,698
encyclopedias, where did it go?

31
00:01:15,348 --> 00:01:17,482
Why don't schools teach it?

32
00:01:17,482 --> 00:01:20,684
Why does it simply vanish from maps in the

33
00:01:20,684 --> 00:01:21,324
1800s?

34
00:01:21,974 --> 00:01:23,787
The second clue is under our feet.

35
00:01:24,437 --> 00:01:26,343
All over the world, there are old

36
00:01:26,343 --> 00:01:28,619
buildings whose ground-floor windows sit

37
00:01:28,619 --> 00:01:29,783
below the street,

38
00:01:30,433 --> 00:01:32,451
half-buried, as if the earth rose up and

39
00:01:32,451 --> 00:01:33,296
swallowed them.

40
00:01:33,946 --> 00:01:36,663
Believers call it the mud flood:

41
00:01:36,663 --> 00:01:39,674
a global catastrophe in the 1800s that

42
00:01:39,674 --> 00:01:42,758
buried Tartarian cities under meters of

43
00:01:42,758 --> 00:01:43,272
mud.

44
00:01:43,922 --> 00:01:46,261
The survivors, they say, simply built on

45
00:01:46,261 --> 00:01:47,240
top and forgot.

46
00:01:47,890 --> 00:01:49,907
The third clue can only be seen from

47
00:01:49,907 --> 00:01:50,372
above.

48
00:01:51,022 --> 00:01:53,480
Star-shaped fortresses, with perfect

49
00:01:53,480 --> 00:01:56,127
geometric points, appear in Europe, the

50
00:01:56,127 --> 00:01:57,954
Americas, Africa and Asia.

51
00:01:58,604 --> 00:02:01,143
Some believers say they're too advanced,

52
00:02:01,143 --> 00:02:03,386
too similar, too widespread to be a

53
00:02:03,386 --> 00:02:04,271
coincidence.

54
00:02:04,921 --> 00:02:06,044
A few go further:

55
00:02:06,044 --> 00:02:08,233
maybe they weren't forts at all, but

56
00:02:08,233 --> 00:02:10,253
machines, built to harness some

57
00:02:10,253 --> 00:02:11,825
forgotten form of energy.

58
00:02:12,475 --> 00:02:14,366
The fourth clue points to the sky.

59
00:02:15,016 --> 00:02:17,740
Look closely at old palaces, churches and

60
00:02:17,740 --> 00:02:20,403
exhibition halls, and you'll see spires,

61
00:02:20,403 --> 00:02:23,189
domes topped with metal points, and towers

62
00:02:23,189 --> 00:02:25,666
crowned with what look like antennas.

63
00:02:26,316 --> 00:02:28,053
Some believers say these weren't

64
00:02:28,053 --> 00:02:29,146
decorations at all.

65
00:02:29,796 --> 00:02:31,926
They say Tartarian buildings were

66
00:02:31,926 --> 00:02:34,353
connected to a worldwide grid of free

67
00:02:34,353 --> 00:02:36,957
energy, pulling electricity straight out

68
00:02:36,957 --> 00:02:38,200
of the atmosphere,

69
00:02:38,850 --> 00:02:41,182
technology later hidden so people would

70
00:02:41,182 --> 00:02:42,570
have to pay for power.

71
00:02:43,220 --> 00:02:45,891
They often point to the inventor Nikola

72
00:02:45,891 --> 00:02:48,501
Tesla, who really did dream of sending

73
00:02:48,501 --> 00:02:51,297
electricity through the air without wires.

74
00:02:51,947 --> 00:02:54,043
The fifth clue is in old photographs.

75
00:02:54,693 --> 00:02:58,135
In 1893, Chicago hosted a World's Fair.

76
00:02:58,135 --> 00:03:01,331
Photos show a dazzling "White City":

77
00:03:01,331 --> 00:03:04,937
palaces, domes and columns, gleaming like

78
00:03:04,937 --> 00:03:05,756
marble.

79
00:03:06,406 --> 00:03:08,596
Then, within a couple of years, almost all

80
00:03:08,596 --> 00:03:09,569
of it was gone.

81
00:03:10,219 --> 00:03:12,550
How, believers ask, could anyone build

82
00:03:12,550 --> 00:03:14,086
such wonders so quickly?

83
00:03:14,086 --> 00:03:16,076
And why would they destroy them?

84
00:03:16,726 --> 00:03:19,026
Unless, they say, the buildings were never

85
00:03:19,026 --> 00:03:21,173
built for the fair at all, but found.

86
00:03:21,823 --> 00:03:23,639
And the sixth clue is the darkest.

87
00:03:24,289 --> 00:03:27,562
Between 1854 and 1929, trains carried

88
00:03:27,562 --> 00:03:31,163
around two hundred thousand children from

89
00:03:31,163 --> 00:03:34,519
crowded eastern American cities to new

90
00:03:34,519 --> 00:03:36,237
homes in the West.

91
00:03:36,887 --> 00:03:38,886
History calls them the orphan trains.

92
00:03:39,536 --> 00:03:42,078
Some Tartaria believers have a chilling

93
00:03:42,078 --> 00:03:44,681
explanation: these children, they claim,

94
00:03:44,681 --> 00:03:47,344
were used to repopulate the world after

95
00:03:47,344 --> 00:03:48,374
the mud flood,

96
00:03:49,024 --> 00:03:51,366
and to raise a new generation that would

97
00:03:51,366 --> 00:03:52,674
never know the truth.

98
00:03:53,324 --> 00:03:55,252
Maps. Mud. Stars.

99
00:03:55,252 --> 00:03:57,566
Spires. White cities.

100
00:03:57,566 --> 00:04:00,169
Trains full of children.

101
00:04:00,819 --> 00:04:02,837
It sounds like a perfect puzzle.

102
00:04:02,837 --> 00:04:05,430
So now, let's do what every good detective

103
00:04:05,430 --> 00:04:06,986
does: check the records.

104
00:04:07,636 --> 00:04:08,923
Start with the maps.

105
00:04:08,923 --> 00:04:10,882
"Tartary" was never one country.

106
00:04:11,532 --> 00:04:14,042
It was what Europeans called the huge part

107
00:04:14,042 --> 00:04:15,938
of Asia they knew little about,

108
00:04:16,588 --> 00:04:19,110
the lands of the Tatars, a loose name

109
00:04:19,110 --> 00:04:21,392
for many Turkic and Mongol peoples.

110
00:04:22,042 --> 00:04:24,651
Medieval writers even linked "Tatar" to

111
00:04:24,651 --> 00:04:27,260
Tartarus, the Greek underworld, as if

112
00:04:27,260 --> 00:04:29,744
the Mongol riders had come from hell.

113
00:04:30,394 --> 00:04:33,639
As explorers and empires mapped Asia in

114
00:04:33,639 --> 00:04:37,038
detail, the vague label was replaced with

115
00:04:37,038 --> 00:04:40,514
real names: Siberia, Kazakhstan, Mongolia.

116
00:04:41,164 --> 00:04:42,621
Tartary didn't vanish.

117
00:04:42,621 --> 00:04:44,020
The blank spot did.

118
00:04:44,670 --> 00:04:46,401
The "mud flood" buildings have

119
00:04:46,401 --> 00:04:47,975
well-recorded explanations.

120
00:04:48,625 --> 00:04:51,661
After the Great Seattle Fire of 1889,

121
00:04:51,661 --> 00:04:54,774
Seattle rebuilt its streets one to two

122
00:04:54,774 --> 00:04:57,735
stories higher to fix flooding and

123
00:04:57,735 --> 00:04:58,646
drainage.

124
00:04:59,296 --> 00:05:01,341
Old ground floors became basements.

125
00:05:01,341 --> 00:05:03,548
You can tour the Seattle Underground

126
00:05:03,548 --> 00:05:04,032
today.

127
00:05:04,682 --> 00:05:07,725
In the 1850s and 60s, Chicago used

128
00:05:07,725 --> 00:05:11,179
thousands of jackscrews to lift entire

129
00:05:11,179 --> 00:05:14,469
buildings to install a sewer system,

130
00:05:15,119 --> 00:05:17,145
sometimes while people kept working

131
00:05:17,145 --> 00:05:17,679
inside.

132
00:05:18,329 --> 00:05:20,849
And everywhere else, streets simply rise

133
00:05:20,849 --> 00:05:23,369
over centuries, as new paving and rubble

134
00:05:23,369 --> 00:05:24,014
pile up.

135
00:05:24,664 --> 00:05:26,362
Star forts were a response to the

136
00:05:26,362 --> 00:05:26,928
cannon.

137
00:05:27,578 --> 00:05:31,064
In Italy in the late 1400s and 1500s,

138
00:05:31,064 --> 00:05:34,201
engineers learned that tall walls

139
00:05:34,201 --> 00:05:36,903
shattered under cannon fire,

140
00:05:37,553 --> 00:05:40,434
while low, thick, angled walls survived,

141
00:05:40,434 --> 00:05:43,381
and pointed bastions left no blind spots.

142
00:05:44,031 --> 00:05:46,019
They wrote books full of diagrams.

143
00:05:46,019 --> 00:05:48,274
France's Vauban built or rebuilt more

144
00:05:48,274 --> 00:05:49,241
than a hundred.

145
00:05:49,891 --> 00:05:52,175
They look alike for the same reason

146
00:05:52,175 --> 00:05:53,557
airports look alike:

147
00:05:53,557 --> 00:05:55,420
same problem, same solution.

148
00:05:56,070 --> 00:05:57,315
And the White City?

149
00:05:57,315 --> 00:05:59,693
Most of its buildings were temporary:

150
00:06:00,343 --> 00:06:02,922
frames covered in staff, a cheap mix of

151
00:06:02,922 --> 00:06:05,442
plaster and fibers, painted to look like

152
00:06:05,442 --> 00:06:06,028
marble.

153
00:06:06,678 --> 00:06:09,748
They were built to last one summer.

154
00:06:09,748 --> 00:06:12,979
A fire in 1894 destroyed much of what

155
00:06:12,979 --> 00:06:13,948
remained.

156
00:06:14,598 --> 00:06:17,177
One building was rebuilt in stone, and

157
00:06:17,177 --> 00:06:19,880
it's now Chicago's Museum of Science and

158
00:06:19,880 --> 00:06:20,635
Industry.

159
00:06:21,285 --> 00:06:24,332
The spires? Many are simply architecture:

160
00:06:24,332 --> 00:06:27,172
bell towers, flagpoles and decoration.

161
00:06:27,822 --> 00:06:30,914
And the metal points on top are often

162
00:06:30,914 --> 00:06:34,161
lightning rods, invented by Benjamin

163
00:06:34,161 --> 00:06:36,171
Franklin in the 1750s,

164
00:06:36,821 --> 00:06:39,485
to carry lightning safely into the ground,

165
00:06:39,485 --> 00:06:41,971
not to pull free energy out of the sky.

166
00:06:42,621 --> 00:06:45,046
As for Tesla, his famous wireless power

167
00:06:45,046 --> 00:06:47,124
tower at Wardenclyffe was never

168
00:06:47,124 --> 00:06:47,816
finished.

169
00:06:48,466 --> 00:06:51,493
His investors pulled out, and the tower

170
00:06:51,493 --> 00:06:53,366
was demolished in 1917.

171
00:06:53,366 --> 00:06:56,537
A real story of ambition and failure, not

172
00:06:56,537 --> 00:06:57,762
a hidden grid.

173
00:06:58,412 --> 00:07:00,682
And the orphan trains are among the

174
00:07:00,682 --> 00:07:03,012
best-documented chapters of American

175
00:07:03,012 --> 00:07:04,087
social history.

176
00:07:04,737 --> 00:07:07,335
The children had names, birth records and

177
00:07:07,335 --> 00:07:09,874
families. Many told their own stories as

178
00:07:09,874 --> 00:07:12,236
adults, and historians have collected

179
00:07:12,236 --> 00:07:14,126
their letters and interviews.

180
00:07:14,776 --> 00:07:17,383
The real story is about poverty and a

181
00:07:17,383 --> 00:07:20,250
controversial child welfare system, not a

182
00:07:20,250 --> 00:07:22,074
secret reset of humanity.

183
00:07:22,724 --> 00:07:25,294
So why does Tartaria feel so convincing?

184
00:07:25,944 --> 00:07:28,276
Psychologist Karen Douglas and her

185
00:07:28,276 --> 00:07:30,728
colleagues suggest conspiracy theories

186
00:07:30,728 --> 00:07:31,925
meet three needs:

187
00:07:32,575 --> 00:07:35,157
to understand a confusing world, to feel

188
00:07:35,157 --> 00:07:37,281
safe and in control, and to feel

189
00:07:37,281 --> 00:07:39,864
special, to know something others don't.

190
00:07:40,514 --> 00:07:43,058
There's also proportionality bias:

191
00:07:43,058 --> 00:07:45,798
we feel that something magnificent must

192
00:07:45,798 --> 00:07:47,624
have a magnificent cause.

193
00:07:48,274 --> 00:07:50,946
Ordinary workers with ordinary tools feel

194
00:07:50,946 --> 00:07:52,645
like too small an answer.

195
00:07:53,295 --> 00:07:56,118
But notice what the theory quietly does:

196
00:07:56,118 --> 00:07:58,809
it takes the real achievements of real

197
00:07:58,809 --> 00:08:01,762
people, architects, stonemasons, laborers,

198
00:08:02,412 --> 00:08:04,621
and gives them to an empire that never

199
00:08:04,621 --> 00:08:05,214
existed.

200
00:08:05,864 --> 00:08:07,277
And here's the twist.

201
00:08:07,277 --> 00:08:09,514
The real lands of "Tartary" had a

202
00:08:09,514 --> 00:08:11,986
history more spectacular than any myth.

203
00:08:12,636 --> 00:08:15,990
In the 1200s, the Mongol Empire became

204
00:08:15,990 --> 00:08:19,110
the largest contiguous land empire in

205
00:08:19,110 --> 00:08:22,307
history, from Korea to Eastern Europe.

206
00:08:22,957 --> 00:08:25,319
After it, the Golden Horde, largely

207
00:08:25,319 --> 00:08:27,916
Turkic-speaking, ruled the western steppe

208
00:08:27,916 --> 00:08:29,746
for more than two centuries.

209
00:08:30,396 --> 00:08:32,745
Its descendants include today's Tatars,

210
00:08:32,745 --> 00:08:34,255
Kazakhs and many others.

211
00:08:34,255 --> 00:08:36,493
And through these lands ran the Silk

212
00:08:36,493 --> 00:08:36,996
Road.

213
00:08:37,646 --> 00:08:39,578
A real giant empire existed.

214
00:08:39,578 --> 00:08:41,822
We just don't need to invent one.

215
00:08:42,472 --> 00:08:44,973
Next time you meet a "hidden history," ask

216
00:08:44,973 --> 00:08:46,028
three questions.

217
00:08:46,678 --> 00:08:48,655
Is there a simpler explanation that's

218
00:08:48,655 --> 00:08:49,841
already written down?

219
00:08:50,491 --> 00:08:52,633
If this were true, what else would we

220
00:08:52,633 --> 00:08:54,614
expect to find, and do we find it?

221
00:08:55,264 --> 00:08:56,929
And who gains if I believe it?

222
00:08:57,579 --> 00:09:00,194
Tartaria is a story about a lost empire.

223
00:09:00,844 --> 00:09:03,562
But maybe what's really lost is our trust

224
00:09:03,562 --> 00:09:06,157
that ordinary people, with simple tools

225
00:09:06,157 --> 00:09:08,813
and enormous effort, built extraordinary

226
00:09:08,813 --> 00:09:09,431
things.

227
00:09:10,081 --> 00:09:12,319
They did. We know their names.

228
00:09:12,969 --> 00:09:15,314
The truth didn't erase a civilization.

229
00:09:15,314 --> 00:09:16,916
It is the civilization.

